=== Psalm 9
--- Psalm 9 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 9
sub: With my whole heart • He shall judge the world in righteousness
refs: Psalm 9:1

--- With my whole heart
kicker: Psalm 9 · Praise
scripture: Psalm 9:1-2 | mark: I will praise thee, O LORD, with my whole heart
p: **Psalm 9** continues the prayerful praise of the Lord, the call for His righteous judgment, destruction of enemies, rebuke of the heathen, and the eternal salvation to come.
p: The first of the two great commandments, *Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind*, is reflected in the praise and worship of David's song. Praising God with our whole heart, acknowledging all His wonderful and marvelous works, rejoicing in them.

--- His yoke is easy
kicker: Application • Praise first
p: Note, David is beset by enemies, his cause is right, yet many are against him, he is oppressed, and yet, David begins with praise and rejoicing.
p: It's been said before, but Jesus does not take away our burdens and cares, He simply makes those burdens light. His yoke is easy. When we are buffeted by the cares and worries of this world, we should stop and praise God for all He has done.
p: Gratitude and praise should be in the front of our minds and love for Him in our hearts, because in the end, His grace, His saving us is sufficient. We are on the winning side. We have overcome the world in Jesus. So sing praise to His name, *O Thou most High.*

--- When mine enemies are turned back
kicker: Psalm 9 · Mine enemies
scripture: Psalm 9:3-4 | mark: When mine enemies are turned back
p: *When mine enemies are turned back*, not if, but when, when they are destroyed by His presence, it is because His enemies are ours.
scripture: Matthew 5:43-44 | mark: Love your enemies

--- Love your enemies
kicker: Psalm 9 · Mine enemies
p: We are called to love our personal enemies. David had multiple opportunities to kill Saul, yet he spared him, and appealed to the King in love.

--- Their memorial is perished
kicker: Psalm 9 · The wicked destroyed
scripture: Psalm 9:5-6 | mark: their memorial is perished with them
p: But those that oppress the righteous, and the wicked full of iniquity, God will judge. And we can absolutely pray imprecatory prayers against them, that God will repay. That His vengeance be done, knowing that in the end, there is no escape for the wicked. Their cities will burn, their line will be cut off, there will be no memory of them, and their soul will be in flames of eternal torment.
p: What little we can do in our wrath? Better to rejoice in the wrath of God, knowing He will handle it.

--- He hath prepared his throne
kicker: Psalm 9 · The throne
scripture: Psalm 9:7; Psalm 9:18 | mark: he hath prepared his throne for judgment; the expectation of the poor shall not perish for ever
p: His throne is established forever in righteousness. He will judge. He will take up the cause of the poor and oppressed. Even in great tribulation, it shall be cut short for the elects' sake.

--- They that know thy name
kicker: Psalm 9 · A refuge
scripture: Psalm 9:9-12 | mark: they that know thy name will put their trust in thee; he forgetteth not the cry of the humble
p: Because we have placed our trust in His holy name, the Lord Jesus, Who will not forsake those that seek Him. He hears the cry of the humble.

--- If we serve Him, we will see trouble
kicker: Application • Trouble
p: There are so many problems we will have in our life, as men on the earth, and persecutions and attacks added on top of them for believers, if we do the works of God. If we serve Him, we will see trouble.
p: How often do we experience or witness the attacks of the devil on believers who turn to God, and do His work?
p: I was talking with a younger sister in Christ who just won someone to the Lord, and how awesome that was. Within a few days, she was being afflicted from all sides by multiple travails and trials.

--- Her light so shine
kicker: Application • Trouble
p: It was good to be able to give her this perspective. To know to patiently endure it, and see her faith grow. To turn to trusting the Lord to see her through it, and to love on those around her so that they will see her light so shine, that they'll praise God in Heaven. That the unsaved might be persuaded by her chaste conversation. Her behavior in the face of adversity.

--- From the gates of death
kicker: Psalm 9 · Have mercy upon me
scripture: Psalm 9:13-14 | mark: thou that liftest me up from the gates of death
p: David's story is a wonderful example of how we should view things. His Psalms help sharpen that focus on God when we are really going through it.

--- Turned into hell
kicker: Psalm 9 · The wicked snared
scripture: Psalm 9:15-17 | mark: the wicked is snared in the work of his own hands; The wicked shall be turned into hell
p: We can humbly ask God for His mercy, and know that He who has lifted us up from the gates of death will save us, while the heathen and enemies of God will be snared by their own sin and turned into Hell. Amen

--- Higgaion. Selah.
kicker: Psalm 9:16 · A brief note
p: Brief note on "Higgaion. Selah."
p: Higgaion is a Hebrew word that means 'meditation,' or something like it, and seems to be a cue for a musical break, heavy harp, where the singers and congregation considered the words just said. It is used just a couple of times in the Psalms.
p: Selah, which is used much more frequently, seems to serve as a pause, perhaps for a musical bridge.
p: The definitions are somewhat educated guess, and opinion. I feel that 'selah' is a bit of a 'think about that,' and 'amen' combined. But I am not a musician, and it may seem more of a way to bring the music back around to those that are.
p: I think it is more important to look at what's being said, and not get caught up in the pedantic. Selah.

--- He shall judge the world in righteousness
kicker: Psalm 9 · Mars' hill
big: Psalm 9:8 | mark: he shall judge the world in righteousness
p: The Apostle Paul quotes **Psalm 9:8** during the Sermon at Mars Hill, where Paul preaches Christ to the heathen.

--- To the unknown God
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 9:8
scripture: Acts 17:22-26 | mark: TO THE UNKNOWN GOD

--- He will judge the world in righteousness
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 9:8
scripture: Acts 17:27-31 | mark: he will judge the world in righteousness by that man whom he hath ordained

--- No excuse in Christ
kicker: Psalm 9 · Let the heathen be judged
scripture: Psalm 9:19-20 | mark: let the heathen be judged in thy sight
p: The cry of David against the heathen, that those who don't believe in the power of the Lord be destroyed, is turned into a warning by Paul to the heathen of Athens. The time of God winking at their pagan idolatry is over. The judgment is here, because in Christ there is no excuse.
p: God was made flesh, died for all men and rose again. That assurance is sure, and every man must come to repentance, trusting on the name of Jesus.

=== Psalm 10
--- Psalm 10 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 10
sub: The wicked in his pride • The helper of the fatherless
refs: Psalm 10:1

--- Why standest thou afar off?
kicker: Psalm 10 · How long?
big: Psalm 10:1 | mark: Why standest thou afar off, O LORD?
p: **Psalm 10** acts as a pair to **Psalm 9**, and a meditation on the wicked, against who David is seeking God's judgment.
p: David opens the Psalm feeling like God's delay in judgment is His hiding Himself, standing afar off, allowing it to continue. We may all feel this way at times. We know God hears us, we have experienced His mercy and blessings and seen His wrath revealed from Heaven, that no man has any excuse to not fear Him...yet, His perfect timing can make us impatient. Even as the saints in Heaven, martyred in tribulation cry out;

--- How long, O Lord, holy and true?
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 10:1
big: Revelation 6:10 | mark: How long, O Lord, holy and true
p: Obviously, the wrath of God pours out soon after, His vengeance is sure. We can trust it will come. But it is hard for our human minds to wait.

--- The wicked in his pride
kicker: Psalm 10 · The wicked
scripture: Psalm 10:2-4 | mark: The wicked, through the pride of his countenance, will not seek after God
p: The wicked, through pride, do not seek God. They boast of their wicked desires, and bless those that join in their covetousness. They persecute the poor with their evil devices. Set snares and nets, dig pits, and scheme mischief against the fatherless and the poor. Their iniquity knows no limit.

--- In the secret places
kicker: Psalm 10 · The wicked
scripture: Psalm 10:5-8 | mark: in the secret places doth he murder the innocent
p: While their murder and violence is obvious, some of the ways of their deceit is less so.

--- As a lion in his den
kicker: Psalm 10 · The net
scripture: Psalm 10:9-10 | mark: He lieth in wait secretly as a lion in his den; when he draweth him into his net
p: The subtle ways of the wicked, the nets they spread, are not only to blatantly rob the poor of whatever meager wealth they have, but to trap them in temptation.

--- The nets they spread
kicker: Application • The net
p: They are the drug dealers, booze peddlers, pimps and whoremongers, pornographers, bookies running gambling parlors, scam artists with get rich quick schemes...all of the ways, big and small that evil men seek to take advantage of those who are struggling, who have no father to guide them, who are desperate to escape their poverty, who are not strong in faith and so are easily caught up in vice.

--- As a roaring lion
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 10:9
p: The description of these men in verse 9 echoes the description of their father, the devil.
big: 1 Peter 5:8 | mark: as a roaring lion
p: These men are as the devil, seeking to take men into their maw, and to bring the righteous to falling, that the adversary can accuse the brethren before God.

--- Thou wilt not require it
kicker: Psalm 10 · Arise, O Lord
scripture: Psalm 10:11-14 | mark: Thou wilt not require it; thou art the helper of the fatherless
p: David, praying to spur action against these men, reminds God of their pride and arrogant way, dismissing any thought of God making recompense against them. They say that He will not require it, He won't make them pay the wages of their sin. That they mock God, so they deserve swift and immediate recompense.

--- The Lord is King for ever
kicker: Psalm 10 · Break thou the arm of the wicked
scripture: Psalm 10:15-16 | mark: The LORD is King for ever and ever
p: David often uses these formulas for the Psalms, praising God, reminding Him Who He is, what He's promised, who the enemy are, what the Lord has done against them in the past, what they deserve now, faith in what He will do to them in the future.

--- To judge the fatherless
kicker: Psalm 10 · The fatherless and the oppressed
scripture: Psalm 10:17-18 | mark: To judge the fatherless and the oppressed
p: Because, God is the judge to the fatherless and defender of the oppressed.

--- Pure religion and undefiled
kicker: Application • The fatherless and the widow
p: The recurring theme of the Lord defending the widow, the fatherless and the poor, and our role in carrying out righteous judgment for them, is probably the most talked about doctrine outside of Who God is, and the doctrines of salvation in Christ.
p: More than anything else, we are to clothe and feed the truly needy, to make sure people don't take advantage of those who have no one to defend them. No husband, no father. To do unto the least of these, and in doing so, doing it for Jesus.
big: James 1:27 | mark: To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction

--- Mercy, and not sacrifice
kicker: Application • The fatherless and the widow
p: This is the ultimate expression of our faith. The outward practice God expects. Self-sacrifice for others, not the selfish sacrifice of the works-based religions of the world.
big: Hosea 6:6 | mark: I desired mercy, and not sacrifice
p: All the penance and "Eucharist" sacrifices, and all the other rites and rituals men do to get the Lord to be pleased with them are vain. God wants us to exercise mercy and judgment.
big: Proverbs 21:3

--- The mission statement
kicker: Application • The fatherless and the widow
p: Jesus, reading Isaiah, gives us the mission statement for Christians;
scripture: Luke 4:18-19 | mark: to preach the gospel to the poor
center: Amen

